      "id": 1,
      "text": "Battle Lake earned its name from a 1795 clash between Ojibwe and Dakota, with the lake later called 'Ish-quan-a-de-win-ing' meaning 'where but few survived'.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/battle_lake_minnesota",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city was platted on October 31, 1881 by Torger and Bertha Olson as the Northern Pacific Railway built a station on their homestead.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_Lake%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Prospect House and Civil War Museum preserves the story of Civil War veteran 'Cap' Colehour, who settled in Battle Lake after the war.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_Lake%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A statue of Ojibwe chief Wenonga stands in Halverson Park, commemorating his leadership during the 1795 battle.",
